@node In Their Own Words
@chapter Quotes — 言葉 (ことば)

@section Luther

@quotation
Here I stand. I can do no other. God help me. Amen.
@author Martin Luther, Diet of Worms, 1521
@end quotation

@quotation
The sin underneath all our sins is the lie of the serpent
that we cannot trust the love and grace of Christ
and must take matters into our own hands.
@author Martin Luther
@end quotation

@section Shinran — 親鸞

@quotation
Even a good person attains birth in the Pure Land,
so it goes without saying that an evil person will.
@author Shinran, Tannisho (歎異抄, たんにしょう), Chapter 3
@end quotation

@noindent
This is Shinran's most radical inversion — parallel to Luther's
@emph{simul justus et peccator} (simultaneously saint and sinner).

@quotation
I, Shinran, have never once uttered the nembutsu for the sake
of my father and mother. The reason is that all beings have been
fathers and mothers, brothers and sisters, in the timeless process
of birth and death.
@author Shinran, Tannisho, Chapter 5
@end quotation

@section Kurosawa — 黒澤明

@quotation
In a mad world, only the mad are sane.
@author Akira Kurosawa
@end quotation

@quotation
Although human beings are incapable of talking about themselves
with total honesty, it is much harder to avoid the truth
while pretending to be other people.
@author Akira Kurosawa
@end quotation

@noindent
And from Seven Samurai (七人の侍, しちにんのさむらい):

@quotation
This is the nature of war: by protecting others, you save yourselves.
If you only think of yourself, you'll only destroy yourself.
@author Kambei (勘兵衛), Seven Samurai, 1954
@end quotation

@c ============================================================
@node Onkochishin
@chapter 温故知新 — The Thread That Binds

@center @strong{温故知新} (おんこちしん)
@center @emph{Study the old to understand the new.}
@center — Confucius, via the Analects (論語, ろんご)

@sp 1

This four-character compound (四字熟語, よじじゅくご) from the Analects
was carried to Japan with Buddhism and Chinese learning.
It became a cornerstone of Japanese intellectual tradition.

Each of our three reformers embodied it:

@table @strong
@item Luther (温, on — to warm, to review)
Returned to the original Greek and Hebrew scriptures,
warming old texts to kindle new understanding.
Onkochishin in action: the Reformation was a @emph{return} to sources.

@item Shinran (故, ko — the old, the past)
Studied twenty years of Tendai tradition on Mount Hiei,
then walked away carrying its deepest truth:
we cannot save ourselves.
The old teaching, honestly received, became something new.

@item Kurosawa (知, chi — to know, to understand)
Drew from Noh theater (能), Shakespeare, Dostoevsky, and Japanese scroll painting.
His films are old stories — a village needs defending, a bureaucrat faces death —
made new through cinema's eye.

@item The Fourth Character (新, shin — the new)
Kikuchiyo (菊千代). The outsider. The one who doesn't fit.
He carries no tradition and invents himself.
He is what the other three made possible:
the new that could not exist without the old.
@end table

@c ============================================================
@node The Keys
@chapter The Keys — 鍵について (かぎについて)

This pamphlet belongs to the @strong{kagichou} (鍵帳) project:
a ledger of hardware security keys named after Kurosawa's samurai.

@table @strong
@item 鍵帳 kagichou (the ledger)
The record of what is. TOML files, registrations, recovery codes tracked.
Like Shinran's letters, like Luther's theses — a written account.

@item 鍵守 kagimori (the guardian)
The Rust tool that will watch over the keys.
Like Kambei surveying the village — seeing what must be protected.
@end table

Four keys bear the four characters:

@example
Kambei    (勘兵衛)  — K51T USB-A   — 温 on   — to warm
Kyuzo     (久蔵)    — K40T USB-C   — 故 ko   — the old
Heihachi  (平八)    — K40T USB-C   — 知 chi  — to know
Kikuchiyo (菊千代)  — K52T USB-C   — 新 shin — the new
@end example

@noindent
Read in sequence: @strong{温 → 故 → 知 → 新}@*
@emph{Warm the old, know the new.}

Three remaining samurai await future keys:
Gorobei (五郎兵衛), Shichiroji (七郎次), Katsushiro (勝四郎).

