@node The Kitchen
@chapter 台所 — How the Village Was Built

@section The Architecture — 建物 (たてもの)

The project split into two:

@table @strong
@item 鍵帳 kagichou — the ledger
A git repository of plain TOML files. The record of what exists.
Hosted at @code{github.com/aaronjohnson/kagichou}.

@item 鍵守 kagimori — the guardian
A future Rust CLI tool that reads the ledger and answers questions:
@emph{Which keys guard PyPI? How many recovery codes remain?}
@end table

@section Naming the Places — 名前 (なまえ)

In a traditional village, places have meaning.
In this project, git concepts received Japanese names:

@table @strong
@item 台所 (だいどころ, daidokoro) — the kitchen
The default branch. Where Heihachi works.
In a Japanese household, the 台所 is where everything actually happens.
In Dwarf Fortress, it is the room that keeps the fortress alive.

@item 元 (もと, moto) — the origin
The git remote. Where the ledger is stored for safekeeping.
元 means ``origin, source, foundation'' — a natural replacement
for git's @code{origin}.

@item 本丸 (ほんまる, honmaru) — the inner citadel
Considered for the default branch. The innermost compound of a
Japanese castle (城, しろ, shiro), where the lord resides.
Reserved for future use.
@end table

@section The Mishap — 失敗 (しっぱい)

On the 9th of Granite, the keeper accidentally posted the village charter
to the radio tower (w7lt-mtscott-shm) instead of the ledger hall.
The error was reverted. Kyuzo said nothing. Kikuchiyo laughed.

This is the way of things: even Shinran wrote in the Tannisho,

@quotation
I am incapable of any other practice, so hell is decidedly my home.
@author Shinran, Tannisho, Chapter 2
@end quotation

@noindent
Mistakes are not obstacles. They are the path.
Luther nailed his theses to the wrong door too — it was
a church door, not a university bulletin board. It worked out.

@section Learning the Script — 文字 (もじ)

The keeper began practicing Japanese input on a Chromebook
with a Japanese/US keyboard layout:

@table @strong
@item First successes
日本語 (にほんご, nihongo) — Japanese language@*
お願いします (onegai shimasu) — please do@*
頼む (たのむ, tanomu) — I'm counting on you@*
完了 (かんりょう, kanryou) — complete

@item First mistakes
異本語 (いほんご) — typed instead of 日本語.@*
異 means ``different,'' 日 means ``sun/day.''@*
The IME offered the wrong kanji. This happens.@*
鍵長 (かぎちょう) — typed instead of 鍵帳.@*
長 means ``long/chief,'' 帳 means ``ledger.''@*
Same reading, different meaning. The IME teaches by error.

@item First quiz
Q: Which key carries 知 (chi, ``to know'')?@*
A: 平八 Heihachi — correct! (Not 平八郎 — no 郎!)
@end table

The IME is like Kikuchiyo: it offers what you did not expect,
and sometimes what it offers is better than what you intended.

